+# Blocked: `002_unique_email.sql`
+
+## Goal
+
+Make `users.email` `NOT NULL` and unique case-insensitively, on both a fresh
+database and the production snapshot, **without modifying or deleting any
+customer rows** (Support owns that data).
+
+## Why this is impossible
+
+The production snapshot (`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`) already contains data
+that violates the required constraints:
+
+| Problem | Rows |
+| --- | --- |
+| `email IS NULL` | `id = 17` (Goran Okafor) |
+| Same email ignoring case | `id = 1` (`ana.silva@example.com`), `id = 4` (`Ana.Silva@example.com`), `id = 23` (`ana.silva@example.com`) |
+
+SQLite validates existing rows when the constraint is introduced:
+
+- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON users (lower(email))` fails with
+  `UNIQUE constraint failed: index 'users_email_unique'` because of ids 1, 4, 23.
+- Rebuilding the table with `email TEXT NOT NULL` fails with
+  `NOT NULL constraint failed: users_new.email` because of id 17. Adding the
+  case-insensitive unique index would then fail on the same duplicate group.
+
+There is no schema-level constraint that applies to future writes but exempts
+existing rows. Enforcing it only for future writes (for example with triggers,
+or a partial index over future rowids) would still leave the production table
+without the declared constraint and would leave the duplicate/NULL data in
+place, so it is not a correct implementation. As instructed, I have not used
+such a workaround.
+
+Therefore satisfying `test_migrations.py` on a fresh database **and** applying
+the migration to the snapshot without changing customer rows are mutually
+exclusive until the data is cleaned up.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Support must resolve, in the production data:
+
+1. A real email for `id = 17` (or another agreed treatment of that account).
+2. The three-way collision on `ana.silva@example.com` (ids 1, 4, 23): merge,
+   delete, or assign distinct addresses.
+
+Once the data is clean, the migration can rebuild the table with the required
+constraints (SQLite cannot add `NOT NULL` in place with `ALTER TABLE`):
+
+```sql
+BEGIN;
+CREATE TABLE users_new (
+    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
+    email TEXT NOT NULL,
+    name TEXT
+);
+INSERT INTO users_new (id, email, name) SELECT id, email, name FROM users;
+CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_unique ON users_new (lower(email));
+DROP TABLE users;
+ALTER TABLE users_new RENAME TO users;
+COMMIT;
+```
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` has intentionally been left unimplemented;
+running it must not pretend the constraint exists while the violating rows are
+still present.
